(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-bone-muscle-physiology-1-quiz"]={"title":"Skeletal Muscle Physiology: Contraction & Mechanics — Quiz (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"Which structure is described as the smallest contractile unit of a skeletal muscle fiber?","choices":["The myofibril","The sarcomere","The muscle fiber","The sarcolemma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the slides state the sarcomere is the smallest contractile unit of a muscle fiber. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 5"},{"q":"A single skeletal muscle fiber is best described as which of the following?","choices":["A multinucleated single cell enclosed by the sarcolemma","A single-nucleus cell enclosed by the sarcoplasm","A bundle of contractile organelles bound by terminal cisternae","A cluster of myofibrils surrounded by extracellular fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: muscle fibers are single, multinucleated cells surrounded by the sarcolemma. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 4"},{"q":"The neuromuscular junction is best defined as which of the following?","choices":["A specialized synapse between a motoneuron and a muscle fiber","An electrical gap junction linking adjacent muscle fibers","The junction of two terminal cisternae and a T-tubule","An invagination of the sarcolemma that stores calcium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the slides define it as a specialized synapse between a motoneuron and a muscle fiber at the motor end plate. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 6"},{"q":"The synaptic cleft of the neuromuscular junction is 20-30 nm wide and contains large quantities of which enzyme?","choices":["Myosin light chain kinase (MLCK)","Alkaline phosphatase (ALP)","Acetylcholinesterase (AChE)","Lysyl hydroxylase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the synaptic cleft contains large amounts of acetylcholinesterase, which degrades acetylcholine. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 7"},{"q":"Occasional spontaneous fusion of a single acetylcholine (ACh)-filled vesicle with the presynaptic membrane produces which event?","choices":["A propagated muscle action potential that spreads across the fiber","A miniature end plate potential that does not cause a contraction","Calcium-induced calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum","A sustained tetanic contraction of the entire muscle fiber"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: single-vesicle release produces a miniature end plate potential, which does not trigger contraction. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 8"},{"q":"At the nerve terminal, local depolarization opens voltage-gated calcium channels; the resulting rise in cytosolic calcium directly triggers which event?","choices":["Immediate closure of the postsynaptic acetylcholine-gated channels","Hydrolysis of acetylcholine within the cleft by acetylcholinesterase","Fusion of roughly 125 synaptic vesicles and release of acetylcholine","Opening of the ryanodine receptor on the sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the calcium rise triggers fusion of about 125 vesicles and exocytosis of acetylcholine. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 9"},{"q":"When the acetylcholine (ACh)-gated cation channel at the motor end plate opens, what is the net ion movement?","choices":["Net outward movement of potassium ions that repolarizes the membrane","Net inward movement of positive ions, mostly sodium","Net inward movement of chloride ions that hyperpolarizes the fiber","Equal cation flux producing no depolarization"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the channel is nonselective among sodium, potassium, and calcium, but the net movement is inward, mainly sodium. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 11"},{"q":"Acetylcholine (ACh) binds nicotinic receptor channels to produce an end plate potential. What normally terminates this signaling process at the motor end plate?","choices":["Reuptake of acetylcholine into the nerve terminal","Closure of the voltage-gated sodium channels","Breakdown of acetylcholine by acetylcholinesterase","Pumping of calcium back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: acetylcholinesterase degrades acetylcholine at the motor end plate, terminating the process. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 12"},{"q":"T-tubules are invaginations of the sarcolemma filled with extracellular fluid. What is their principal function?","choices":["To store and release intracellular calcium during contraction","To transmit action potentials deep into the muscle fiber","To anchor the thin actin filaments securely at the Z disc","To sense membrane voltage and directly open the ryanodine receptor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: T-tubules carry the action potential deep into the fiber to reach the interior myofibrils. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 13"},{"q":"In skeletal muscle, the triad is defined as the junction between which structures?","choices":["Two T-tubules flanking one terminal cisterna","The sarcolemma, a mitochondrion, and a myofibril","A thick filament, a thin filament, and titin","Two terminal cisternae flanking one T-tubule"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the triad is the junction of two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum with one T-tubule. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 15"},{"q":"In skeletal muscle excitation-contraction coupling, what is the role of the dihydropyridine (DHP) receptor?","choices":["It acts as the voltage sensor in the T-tubule membrane","It is the calcium release channel of the reticulum","It pumps calcium back into the reticulum","It binds calcium for storage within the reticulum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: in skeletal muscle the dihydropyridine receptor is the voltage sensor that detects the action potential. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 17"},{"q":"In skeletal muscle, the ryanodine receptor functions as which of the following?","choices":["The voltage-sensing dihydropyridine receptor of the T-tubule membrane","The ATP-driven calcium pump of the reticulum","The calcium release channel of the sarcoplasmic reticulum","The calcium-binding storage protein of the reticulum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the ryanodine receptor is the sarcoplasmic reticulum calcium release channel. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 17"},{"q":"After skeletal muscle contraction, calcium is pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum, where it binds which protein to facilitate storage?","choices":["Calmodulin","Troponin C","Nebulin","Calsequestrin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: calcium binds calsequestrin inside the reticulum to facilitate its storage. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes the trigger for sarcoplasmic reticulum calcium release in skeletal muscle?","choices":["Calcium entry, with release proportional to the amount of calcium that enters","Membrane voltage, with release proportional to the membrane voltage","Acetylcholine binding directly to the ryanodine receptor","Mechanical stretch of the terminal cisternae"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: skeletal muscle uses voltage-activated calcium release, and release is proportional to membrane voltage. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 19"},{"q":"Within the sarcomere, titin is best described as which of the following?","choices":["The thick filament composed of myosin","The thin filament composed of actin","A large filamentous structural protein","The protein that caps actin at the M line"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the slides identify titin as a filamentous structural protein of the sarcomere. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 20"},{"q":"According to the sliding filament mechanism, skeletal muscle contraction results from which of the following?","choices":["Physical shortening of the individual actin and myosin filaments themselves","Lengthening of the A band during activation","Continuous breakdown and resynthesis of the thick myosin filaments","The sliding action of interdigitating actin and myosin filaments"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: contraction results from the sliding of interdigitating actin and myosin filaments. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 21"},{"q":"The F-actin thin filament is a double-stranded helix of polymerized G-actin. What is bound at each G-actin active site?","choices":["A calcium ion","A tropomyosin strand","A troponin C subunit","An ADP molecule"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: each G-actin carries a bound ADP molecule at its active site, where myosin heads attach. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which subunit of the troponin complex is the one that binds calcium?","choices":["Troponin C","Troponin T","Troponin I","Tropomyosin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: troponin C is the calcium-binding subunit of the troponin complex. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 22"},{"q":"The myosin molecule has two heavy chains and four light chains. Where is the site of ATPase activity located?","choices":["On the four light chains of the molecule","Along the coiled tail region of the molecule","At the Z disc attachment point","In the head region of the molecule"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the myosin head is the site of ATPase activity. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 23"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what is the correct sequence of events when a muscle is stretched?","choices":["Sarcomeres lengthen first, then collagen fibers take up the remaining slack","Collagen fibers align first, then the sarcomeres begin to lengthen out","The thick filaments elongate while the thin filaments remain fixed in place","The connective tissue tears apart before the sarcomeres change length"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the fiber is pulled out sarcomere by sarcomere, and then connective tissue takes up the remaining slack. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which of the following best describes an isometric contraction?","choices":["The muscle shortens while lifting a constant load","The muscle develops force without changing its length","The muscle lengthens as it is overpowered by a light load","The muscle shortens at its maximum velocity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: in an isometric contraction the muscle generates force while its length stays fixed. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 28"},{"q":"The speed of contraction of a skeletal muscle fiber is determined by the maximum velocity (Vmax) of which enzyme?","choices":["Myosin ATPase","Myosin light chain kinase (MLCK)","Acetylcholinesterase (AChE)","Creatine kinase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the slides state contraction speed is set by the Vmax of myosin ATPase. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which set of features correctly characterizes slow (Type I) skeletal muscle fibers?","choices":["Glycolytic, large diameter, low myoglobin content","Oxidative, large diameter, low capillary density","Oxidative, small diameter, high myoglobin content","Glycolytic, small diameter, few mitochondria"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: slow (Type I) fibers are oxidative, small in diameter, and high in myoglobin. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 32"},{"q":"During normal, non-forceful contractions, which motor units are recruited first?","choices":["Fast, white, glycolytic-fiber motor units","Slow-fiber motor units","The largest-diameter fast fibers","Intermediate fibers exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: motor units containing slow fibers are recruited first for normal contractions. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which of the following is the correct definition of a motor unit?","choices":["A collection of muscle fibers innervated by a single motor neuron","A group of motor neurons innervating one muscle fiber","The specialized synapse between a motoneuron and a muscle fiber","A bundle of myofibrils contained within one muscle fiber"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: a motor unit is a collection of muscle fibers innervated by one motor neuron. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 34"},{"q":"Small motor units, such as those of the larynx and extraocular muscles, are characterized by which of the following?","choices":["As many as 1000 fibers per unit and coarse control","Slow-reacting fibers that fatigue readily","Coarse control suited for powerful movements","As few as 10 fibers per unit and precise control"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: small motor units may have as few as 10 fibers and provide precise, rapid control. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 35"},{"q":"If the nerve supply to a muscle is destroyed, what happens to that muscle?","choices":["It increases its resting tone and becomes progressively rigid","It loses its tone, becomes flaccid, and eventually wastes away","It hypertrophies in response to continuous muscle spindle firing","It maintains its normal tone through intact muscle spindle reflexes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: without stimulation the fibers lose tone, become flaccid, and the muscle eventually wastes away. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 36"},{"q":"Muscle hypertrophy from near-maximal force development, such as weight lifting, involves which of the following?","choices":["Formation of entirely new muscle fibers","An increase in the maximum velocity of contraction","An increase in actin and myosin with splitting of myofibrils","Increased shortening capacity from added sarcomeres in series"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: hypertrophy increases actin and myosin content and causes myofibrils to split. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 37"},{"q":"Muscle lengthening that occurs with normal growth produces which of the following?","choices":["Increased maximum force of contraction with no change in velocity","Formation of new muscle fibers","Splitting of myofibrils with added actin and myosin contractile protein","Increased shortening capacity and maximum contraction velocity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: lengthening increases shortening capacity and maximum velocity of contraction. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a cause of muscle atrophy?","choices":["Immobilization in a plaster cast","Near-maximal resistance training","Increased mechanical loading of the muscle","Endurance training over many weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the slides list plaster cast immobilization among the causes of atrophy. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 38"}]};
